{
  "id": "Compound::DB00773 - Disease::DOID:2531",
  "verbalization": "Etoposide, a topoisomerase inhibitor, is clinically associated with the treatment of hematologic cancers through its ability to disrupt DNA replication and cause cell death. This relationship is supported by its inclusion in the topoisomerase inhibitor class, which is known to target topoisomerase II, a critical enzyme in DNA repair and cell division. The shared ancestry with Daunorubicin, Idarubicin, and Doxorubicin, all of which are topoisomerase II inhibitors, further reinforces the mechanistic link. These compounds are widely used in the treatment of hematologic malignancies, suggesting a consistent biological role in this context. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of topoisomerase inhibition in the pathogenesis of hematologic cancers, with Etoposide potentially contributing through similar mechanisms. The evidence underscores the role of topoisomerase-targeting agents in the treatment of hematologic disorders, emphasizing the need for precise and mechanistically grounded therapeutic strategies.",
